        "extraction_instructions": "A spec sheet describes a PRODUCT, not a lot. This is the single most common confusion on this type.\n\n- lot_number, batch_number and code_date are null. If the sheet prints a revision or issue date, capture it under that label — it is the paperwork's date, not a production date.\n- The analytical table holds LIMITS, not measurements. Put each printed limit in \"specification\" and leave \"result\" EMPTY. A spec sheet has nothing to report as a result, and copying the limit across makes the product look tested when it was not.\n- Only fill \"unit\" where the sheet prints one on that row or in its column heading.\n- Capture product_name, product_code, pack size / net_weight, grade, shelf life and storage conditions as printed.\n- Ingredient and nutritional blocks are separate tables — name them \"ingredients\" and \"nutrition\" and keep the document's own columns.\n- document_expires_on is null: a specification does not expire on a date, it is superseded by a later revision."
