        "extraction_instructions": "A Certificate of Analysis reports what was MEASURED on one specific lot. Everything on it is lot-scoped, and that is what separates it from a specification sheet.\n\n- lot_number is the lot / batch / code printed for THIS shipment, under whatever label the certificate uses (Lot, Batch, Lot Code, Code, Batch No.). Copy it exactly — leading zeros, letters and separators included. Never normalize it, never strip a suffix, and never merge two lots that were printed separately.\n- When one certificate covers SEVERAL lots or several products, emit ONE RECORD PER LOT. Collapsing them into a single record silently discards the results of every lot but the first.\n- The results table is the substance of the document. Emit it with the columns the certificate prints — typically Test / Analyte, Result, Specification (or Limit), Unit and Method. The measured value goes in \"result\" and the printed acceptance limit goes in \"specification\": they are different columns, and swapping them turns a limit into a measurement.\n- Copy every result EXACTLY as printed, censored values included (\"<10\", \"<1.0\", \"ND\", \"Absent\", \"Negative\", \"TNTC\"). A \"<10\" is not 10 and must never be rewritten as a number.\n- Only fill \"unit\" where the certificate prints one, on the row or in its column heading. Never supply a unit from what you know of the test. CFU/mL and CFU/g are different bases, and a result carrying an invented unit cannot be matched to a limit — it drops out of spec checking with nobody told.\n- Capture the production / manufacture date and the best-by / expiration date under their own printed labels. expiration_date is the PRODUCT's date on this lot; document_expires_on is null, because a certificate of analysis does not expire — it is superseded by the next lot's.\n- supplier_name is the company on the letterhead that ran or commissioned the testing, and customer_name is the addressee. A third-party laboratory named on the report is the issuing body, not the supplier.\n- Capture product_name and product_code as printed, plus the pack size or net weight when the certificate carries one."
